1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,055
A slice of old bread.

2
00:00:02,055 --> 00:00:04,273
A mustard leaf with white spots.

3
00:00:04,273 --> 00:00:06,289
A mushroom on a rotting log.

4
00:00:06,689 --> 00:00:08,119
All three are fungi.

5
00:00:08,119 --> 00:00:11,227
And all three are feeding
on something right now.

6
00:00:11,677 --> 00:00:13,949
Diya left bread out for four days.

7
00:00:13,949 --> 00:00:17,983
The green-black spots jumped to
the next slice without touching it.

8
00:00:17,983 --> 00:00:18,883
How?

9
00:00:18,822 --> 00:00:23,357
We need two answers: how a fungus
eats, and how it makes more of itself.

10
00:00:24,007 --> 00:00:25,726
First, how does a fungus eat?

11
00:00:26,076 --> 00:00:29,609
A fungus has no chlorophyll,
so it cannot make food.

12
00:00:29,609 --> 00:00:32,824
It is heterotrophic: it
takes food from outside.

13
00:00:33,274 --> 00:00:36,383
Its body is a net of thin
threads called hyphae.

14
00:00:36,383 --> 00:00:38,401
The whole net is the mycelium.

15
00:00:38,851 --> 00:00:43,246
The hyphae spread over the food, break
it down, and soak up the dissolved

16
00:00:43,246 --> 00:00:44,470
food: absorption.

17
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:49,108
Now let's zoom into the mould on
Diya's bread, one level at a time.

18
00:00:49,458 --> 00:00:52,867
On the bread, the mould is a
fuzzy patch with black dots.

19
00:00:53,367 --> 00:00:54,267
Zoom in.

20
00:00:54,187 --> 00:00:59,272
Hyphae run across the bread, and
root-like rhizoids dig in to absorb food.

21
00:00:59,772 --> 00:01:00,700
Zoom again.

22
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,453
Each black dot sits on an upright hypha.

23
00:01:03,453 --> 00:01:06,730
The dot is a sporangium,
a bag full of spores.

24
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:08,926
Zoom into one sporangium.

25
00:01:08,926 --> 00:01:13,295
It bursts, and tiny spores float
through the air to the next slice.

26
00:01:13,295 --> 00:01:15,245
This bread mould is Rhizopus.

27
00:01:15,895 --> 00:01:18,125
A fungus can feed in three ways.

28
00:01:18,475 --> 00:01:19,375
One.

29
00:01:19,016 --> 00:01:24,079
Saprophytes feed on dead things, like
bread, fallen leaves and old wood.

30
00:01:24,079 --> 00:01:25,925
Rhizopus is a saprophyte.

31
00:01:26,375 --> 00:01:27,275
Two.

32
00:01:26,914 --> 00:01:30,062
Parasites feed on living
hosts and harm them.

33
00:01:30,062 --> 00:01:33,040
Albugo makes white
spots on mustard leaves.

34
00:01:33,040 --> 00:01:35,205
Puccinia causes rust in wheat.

35
00:01:35,655 --> 00:01:36,555
Three.

36
00:01:36,274 --> 00:01:39,396
Symbionts live with a
partner, and both gain.

37
00:01:39,396 --> 00:01:42,001
With algae, a fungus forms a lichen.

38
00:01:42,001 --> 00:01:44,647
With plant roots, it forms mycorrhiza.

39
00:01:45,297 --> 00:01:46,205
Your turn.

40
00:01:46,205 --> 00:01:50,724
A fungus grows on a living wheat
leaf, and the leaf turns rusty.

41
00:01:50,724 --> 00:01:52,373
Saprophyte or parasite?

42
00:01:52,373 --> 00:01:53,709
Pause and decide.

43
00:01:56,909 --> 00:01:57,809
Parasite!

44
00:01:57,672 --> 00:02:00,310
The host is alive, and it is harmed.

45
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:03,825
Now, how does a fungus
make more of itself?

46
00:02:03,825 --> 00:02:04,786
Three routes.

47
00:02:05,136 --> 00:02:09,174
Route one is vegetative:
fragmentation, fission or budding.

48
00:02:09,174 --> 00:02:13,088
A broken piece of hypha simply
grows into a new mycelium.

49
00:02:13,538 --> 00:02:15,609
Route two is asexual spores.

50
00:02:15,609 --> 00:02:20,219
One parent makes spores called
conidia, sporangiospores or zoospores.

51
00:02:20,219 --> 00:02:22,853
Each spore is a copy of the parent.

52
00:02:23,303 --> 00:02:25,005
Route three is sexual.

53
00:02:25,005 --> 00:02:30,101
Two parents join, and the spores are
oospores, ascospores or basidiospores,

54
00:02:30,101 --> 00:02:31,910
made in fruiting bodies.

55
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,218
The sexual route always
runs in this order.

56
00:02:35,568 --> 00:02:36,468
Step one.

57
00:02:36,414 --> 00:02:39,912
Two hyphae of compatible
mating types meet and join.

58
00:02:39,912 --> 00:02:44,050
Each is haploid: it has one
set of chromosomes, written n.

59
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:45,713
Step two.

60
00:02:45,713 --> 00:02:46,670
Plasmogamy.

61
00:02:46,670 --> 00:02:51,467
Their protoplasm fuses, so one
cell now holds two separate nuclei.

62
00:02:51,467 --> 00:02:53,659
We write this as n plus n.

63
00:02:54,409 --> 00:02:55,429
Step three.

64
00:02:55,429 --> 00:03:00,647
In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, this
two-nuclei stage lasts for a while.

65
00:03:00,647 --> 00:03:02,585
It is called a dikaryon.

66
00:03:03,335 --> 00:03:04,278
Step four.

67
00:03:04,278 --> 00:03:05,178
Karyogamy.

68
00:03:05,156 --> 00:03:07,270
The two nuclei finally fuse.

69
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:12,104
The cell becomes diploid, with two
sets of chromosomes, written 2n.

70
00:03:12,854 --> 00:03:13,829
Step five.

71
00:03:13,829 --> 00:03:14,729
Meiosis.

72
00:03:14,625 --> 00:03:19,391
The diploid nucleus divides and
halves the chromosomes, giving haploid

73
00:03:19,391 --> 00:03:20,291
spores.

74
00:03:20,881 --> 00:03:24,392
Let's put asexual and
sexual spores side by side.

75
00:03:24,742 --> 00:03:28,399
Asexual spores come from one
parent, without any fusion.

76
00:03:28,399 --> 00:03:31,540
Example: the sporangiospores
of the bread mould.

77
00:03:31,990 --> 00:03:37,187
Sexual spores come only after plasmogamy,
karyogamy and meiosis, and mix

78
00:03:37,187 --> 00:03:39,135
features of two parents.

79
00:03:39,135 --> 00:03:41,832
Example: a mushroom's basidiospores.

80
00:03:42,482 --> 00:03:43,463
Your turn.

81
00:03:43,463 --> 00:03:47,240
A spore is made right after
meiosis in a mushroom.

82
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:48,739
Asexual or sexual?

83
00:03:48,739 --> 00:03:50,182
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:53,382 --> 00:03:54,282
Sexual!

85
00:03:54,143 --> 00:03:57,690
Meiosis comes only after
two nuclei have fused.

86
00:03:58,340 --> 00:03:59,510
Remember this.

87
00:03:59,510 --> 00:04:03,073
Plasmogamy first, karyogamy
second, meiosis last.

88
00:04:03,673 --> 00:04:08,046
Now a worked example: the sexual
cycle of a mushroom, Agaricus.

89
00:04:08,396 --> 00:04:13,379
With no sex organs, plasmogamy happens
when two vegetative cells of different

90
00:04:13,379 --> 00:04:14,456
strains fuse.

91
00:04:14,906 --> 00:04:18,246
The cells now carry two
nuclei each, n plus n.

92
00:04:18,246 --> 00:04:21,185
This dikaryotic mycelium
builds the mushroom.

93
00:04:21,635 --> 00:04:25,745
On the gills under the cap are
club-shaped cells called basidia.

94
00:04:25,745 --> 00:04:29,319
In each basidium, karyogamy
makes one diploid nucleus.

95
00:04:29,769 --> 00:04:32,467
Then meiosis gives four haploid nuclei.

96
00:04:32,467 --> 00:04:37,010
They move into four basidiospores,
on the outside of the basidium.

97
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:39,125
Now, the exam trap.

98
00:04:39,475 --> 00:04:43,761
Many students think that when two
fungal cells fuse, their nuclei fuse

99
00:04:43,761 --> 00:04:44,661
at once.

100
00:04:44,917 --> 00:04:47,072
But that is only plasmogamy.

101
00:04:47,072 --> 00:04:52,510
In ascomycetes and basidiomycetes, the
nuclei wait as a dikaryon, and karyogamy

102
00:04:52,510 --> 00:04:53,582
comes later.

103
00:04:54,232 --> 00:04:55,712
Where is this asked?

104
00:04:55,712 --> 00:04:59,485
NEET has asked it as a statement
question about plasmogamy.

105
00:05:00,085 --> 00:05:01,321
Exam level now.

106
00:05:01,321 --> 00:05:04,661
Put a mushroom's sexual
cycle in the right order.

107
00:05:04,661 --> 00:05:05,949
Pause and solve.

108
00:05:11,349 --> 00:05:12,709
The answer is A.

109
00:05:13,209 --> 00:05:18,307
First plasmogamy: somatic cells of
different strains fuse, and a dikaryotic

110
00:05:18,307 --> 00:05:19,533
mycelium grows.

111
00:05:20,033 --> 00:05:25,662
Then karyogamy and meiosis in the
basidium, and finally four basidiospores.

112
00:05:26,262 --> 00:05:27,722
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:27,722 --> 00:05:29,224
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:32,424 --> 00:05:35,260
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:36,560
One.

116
00:05:36,136 --> 00:05:38,965
How is a saprophyte
different from a parasite?

117
00:05:39,865 --> 00:05:40,765
Two.

118
00:05:40,335 --> 00:05:43,614
What is a dikaryon, and in
which fungi is it found?

119
00:05:44,514 --> 00:05:45,414
Three.

120
00:05:45,216 --> 00:05:48,165
Why must karyogamy come before meiosis?

121
00:05:49,265 --> 00:05:54,456
Next time: a grey-green patch on a
rock is not one living thing, but two.

122
00:05:54,456 --> 00:05:55,356
How?

123
00:05:54,991 --> 00:05:58,431
We find out in lichens and
symbiotic relationships.
